WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.909 --> 00:00:02.260
What is the processes

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:04.020
that they go through
in their particular

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.620
cultural space to cope

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:07.620
when there's adversity?

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:12.920
You know, protective factors or the mechanisms,
the processes that systems at different levels

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.270
whether that's an education system, or an
individual child, or indeed even a political

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:18.270
system?

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.460
I'm interested in how

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.800
people perceive their
own resilience

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.050
and how they understand their

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:26.140
capacity and their agency

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.120
to deal with different
types of shocks,

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.539
and disturbances, and to

00:00:29.539 --> 00:00:31.440
actually affect change you know,

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.190
in positive
ways.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:37.420
You can either ask individuals to change,
but actually the truth is that you can also

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:41.890
help people through a lot of period of change
by changing the environments around them,

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:48.920
by creating more facilitative communities,
better social policies, better transportation systems.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.440
It's less of a burden on the individual to
wake up every morning and say okay, I've got

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.740
to cope with this new world because that new
world is just going to facilitate that gentle

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.109
nudge in the right direction.

00:00:59.109 --> 00:01:06.650
I think that CIFAR gives us this great opportunity
to tackle some of these big, global challenges

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:13.250
and it also is really encouraging us to be
quite bold and to really move outside our

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:19.050
comfort zones and I think that some of the
intractable problems that we've been talking

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:25.050
about over the last few days where we think
ah, coming together and bringing these different

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:31.220
disciplinary perspectives and developing this
multi-systemic approach can really help to

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.300
answer some of these tricky questions.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.771
And suddenly you're talking about these very
complex models of watching systems adapt,

00:01:37.771 --> 00:01:42.320
and transform when they're under stress so
that they are more sustainable and indeed

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.660
more promoting of health and wellbeing.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:52.100
[Music]

